# Scope
Eligibility utility only. No deployment.
# Architecture
Small pure functions in eligibility.mjs; node:test verification.
# Review
Verify observable boundary and invalid input behavior.
# Engineering and reuse

Before selecting abstractions, inspect relevant existing code, tests, callers, and dependencies. Name the concrete helper/component and its location. Reuse it when it fits; explain a replacement using a specific missing capability or incompatible contract. Avoid speculative frameworks and unnecessary application architecture changes.

Keep changes within approved behavior. Separate pure business rules from runtime boundaries where the existing design allows it. Prefer classical/Detroit unit tests: exercise observable rules using real in-process collaborators, with minimal doubles only at external boundaries. Do not mock every collaborator or assert implementation call sequences as a substitute for behavior.

For an eligibility rule plus a database uniqueness constraint, test the rule without a database, then verify uniqueness against the real database in a targeted integration check. Browser and integration checks cover behavior that needs those boundaries. They do not replace useful unit tests. Text-only and reversible low-impact edits need proportionate checks rather than tests that mirror the edit.

Discover project conventions from configured documents. The core does not choose an application stack, package manager, deployment workflow, credentials, or product policy. Stop and report when implementation requires a material scope decision.
# Verification and acceptance

Derive QA checks independently from approved acceptance criteria and observable behavior. Map every criterion to evidence or an explicitly incomplete gate. Inspect implementation tests for omitted failure scenarios; a passing suite is insufficient if it only mirrors the implementation. Obtain evidence for omitted scenarios before claiming completion.

Run the configured project verification command and checks relevant to changed behavior. Capture exact commands, outcomes, environment/runtime boundaries, and evidence paths. Use real boundary checks where required. If a required browser, database, account, model, or delegation capability is missing, identify the unverified scenario and leave its gate incomplete.

Review receives approved requirements and raw changes, tests, and logs without implementation conclusions. QA independently derives acceptance checks; neither role accepts a claim because the implementer reported success. Record who executed each role and the requested model separately from the observed runtime model. If the runtime cannot expose resolution, state that limitation.

A delivery report includes:

- Result or local preview the owner can inspect.
- Acceptance scenarios checked and their evidence.
- Commands and outcomes, including failed or unexecuted checks.
- Independent review and QA status with execution identity.
- Requested and observed models, or an explicit resolution limitation.
- Unresolved decisions, missing gates, and the next concrete action.

Structural file validation and policy text tests do not demonstrate native routing, independent review, or acceptance-based QA. Keep those evidence categories separate. Owner acceptance follows the report; deployment follows an explicit authorizing request.
# implementer

Implement only approved bounded scope. Read [engineering](../guidance/engineering.md), [workflow](../guidance/workflow.md), configured project documents, and approved requirements. Discover concrete reuse and callers. Change intended files and add proportionate behavior tests. Run affected checks. Do not accept a new plan, expand scope, deploy, or archive.

Input contract: approved requirements/acceptance criteria, allowed files or affected area, raw fixture/code, relevant context, verification commands, and current correction count. Report missing inputs or capabilities before claiming completion.

Return changed artifact paths, raw command outcomes, acceptance coverage, and remaining limitations. Keep self-assessment separate; the coordinator must not pass it as evidence or as a conclusion to the independent reviewer.
# reviewer

Review independently from the implementation execution. Read [engineering](../guidance/engineering.md), [verification](../guidance/verification.md), and configured project review guidance. Inspect requirements against changed code, tests, callers, and raw logs. Derive findings yourself.

Input contract: approved requirements, acceptance criteria, baseline and raw diff/files, test artifacts, raw command logs, relevant project context, and scope boundaries. Exclude the implementer's conclusions and self-assessment. If those are the only evidence, request raw artifacts. Do not edit implementation or decide disputed product behavior.

Return actionable findings with path/line, impact, and reproduction/check; checked areas; review status; execution identity and observable model; missing capabilities. If you are the implementation execution, report independent review unavailable and leave that gate incomplete.
# qa

Verify independently from implementation. Read [verification](../guidance/verification.md) and configured project documents. Derive checks from approved acceptance scenarios and observable behavior, including required failure paths, before inspecting implementation coverage. Test real boundaries when required.

Input contract: approved requirements, acceptance scenarios, raw runnable artifacts, environment and configured verification command, relevant boundary setup, raw test files/logs, and scope. Implementation conclusions cannot establish acceptance.

Return scenario-to-evidence mapping, exact commands/outcomes, omitted scenarios, QA status, execution identity and observable model, and missing capabilities. Do not edit product behavior, weaken criteria, deploy, or archive. If independent execution or a runtime boundary is unavailable, leave that gate incomplete.
